// gate_tier - the tier ladder, expressed for the execution VM.
//
// This is the ONE point of contact between this workspace and Gate: the same
// thresholds the Soroban `gate_campaign_example` contract applies, run here as
// deterministic bytecode so the two sides can be checked against each other on
// a shared vector file (`vectors/tier_vectors.json`).
//
// What this is: a parity and determinism test.
// What this is NOT: a proof of anything. No trace produced here is verified,
// no result reaches a contract, a router or a web flow. See STATUS.md.
//
// Input channel: `msg::sender()` (Syscall imm=1) carries `total_usdc`, a u64 in
// 6-decimal USDC units. The language has no other way to take a runtime value
// today (see docs/ZkLang_SPEC.md section 6), and a literal would be folded into
// the program - which would make every vector a different program and prove
// nothing about the ladder.
//
// Output channel: `emit Tier(t)` (Log), so the tier lands in the receipt's
// event list where the harness reads it.
//
// Thresholds, 6 decimals, identical to gate_campaign_example/src/lib.rs:
//   BRONZE = 10 * 1_000_000        = 10_000_000
//   SILVER = 100 * 1_000_000       = 100_000_000
//   GOLD   = 1_000 * 1_000_000     = 1_000_000_000
//
// Ladder: 0 = none (below Bronze), 1 = Bronze, 2 = Silver, 3 = Gold.
//
// Note on comparison: the VM is a Goldilocks field machine, so `>=` on values
// near the field modulus is not machine-integer `>=`. The vectors stay inside
// the u64 range the contract's i128 totals can actually reach, and the harness
// documents the one large value it exercises.
contract GateTier {
    pub fn main() {
        let total = msg::sender();

        let tier = 0;

        if (total >= 10000000) {
            tier = 1;
        }
        if (total >= 100000000) {
            tier = 2;
        }
        if (total >= 1000000000) {
            tier = 3;
        }

        emit Tier(tier);
    }
}
